<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} date={date} readTime={readTime} keywords={keywords}>
  <p>Colour is the first thing people notice about your brand. Studies show that up to 90% of snap judgments about products are based on colour alone. Choose the wrong palette, and you will confuse your audience. Choose the right one, and you will communicate your values before a single word is read.</p>

  <p>But how do you actually choose? This article presents a systematic, repeatable process for selecting brand colours that communicate the right message, examines how colour psychology varies by industry, provides a framework for competitive analysis, and shows you how to evolve your palette over time without losing recognition.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: Define Your Brand Personality</h2>

  <p>Before opening a colour picker, write down 3-5 adjectives that describe your brand. Are you playful or serious? Luxurious or accessible? Traditional or disruptive? These words become your North Star — every colour decision should reinforce them.</p>

  <p>Consider competitor palettes too. If every company in your space uses blue (hello, fintech), a bold orange or teal might help you stand out. Differentiation matters.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: Understand Your Audience</h2>

  <p>Different demographics respond differently to colour. Age, gender, culture, and industry all play roles:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Younger audiences</strong> tend to prefer brighter, more saturated colours.</li>
    <li><strong>Older demographics</strong> often respond better to muted, sophisticated tones.</li>
    <li><strong>Gender associations</strong> vary by culture — do not assume pink is "for women" universally.</li>
    <li><strong>Industry norms</strong> create expectations: green for sustainability, blue for finance, red for food.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 3: Choose Your Dominant Colour</h2>

  <p>Your dominant colour is your brand's signature. It appears in your logo, primary buttons, and key marketing materials. This colour should:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Reflect your brand personality (Step 1)</li>
    <li>Resonate with your target audience (Step 2)</li>
    <li>Be distinctive within your competitive landscape</li>
    <li>Work at small sizes (favicon, app icon) and large (billboards)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Spend time here. A strong dominant colour anchors your entire visual identity.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: Build Supporting Colours</h2>

  <p>Once you have your dominant colour, use colour theory to find harmonious supporting colours:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Analogous</strong> colours create cohesion and calm — ideal for backgrounds and secondary elements.</li>
    <li><strong>Complementary</strong> colours provide contrast for calls-to-action and highlights.</li>
    <li><strong>Monochromatic</strong> variations (tints and shades) add depth without introducing new hues.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A typical brand palette includes 3-5 colours: one dominant, one or two secondary, and one or two neutrals.</p>

  <h2>Step 5: Add Functional Neutrals</h2>

  <p>Neutrals are the unsung heroes of brand palettes. They handle text, backgrounds, borders, and subtle UI elements. A well-designed neutral system includes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Text black:</strong> A warm or cool dark grey rather than pure #000000 — it prints better and feels less harsh on screens.</li>
    <li><strong>Background white:</strong> Often slightly off-white (#FAFAFA or #F5F5F5) to reduce eye strain.</li>
    <li><strong>Border grey:</strong> A light grey for dividers and subtle boundaries.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 6: Test Across Media</h2>

  <p>Colours behave differently on screens versus paper, and differently on glossy versus matte finishes. Always:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Test digital colours on multiple devices (calibrated monitor, phone, tablet)</li>
    <li>Print physical swatches for packaging or stationery</li>
    <li>Check how colours photograph under different lighting</li>
  </ul>

  <p>What looks vibrant on a Retina display may look muddy in print. Plan for both.</p>

  <h2>Document and Lock Your Palette</h2>

  <p>Once chosen, document your colours in a brand guidelines document with:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hex codes for digital use</li>
    <li>RGB values for screen design</li>
    <li>CMYK values for print</li>
    <li>Pantone references for premium print</li>
    <li>Usage rules (what goes where)</li>
    <li>Accessibility notes (minimum contrast ratios)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Rebrand: Signs Your Palette Needs Refreshing</h2>

  <p>Even timeless palettes eventually need updating. Here are the signals that it is time:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your competitors have converged on your colours:</strong> If three new entrants launched with palettes nearly identical to yours, differentiation has eroded.</li>
    <li><strong>Your audience has shifted:</strong> A brand targeting Gen Z in 2020 may need different colours in 2030 as that cohort ages and tastes evolve.</li>
    <li><strong>Your product has changed:</strong> A B2B SaaS company pivoting to consumer may need warmer, more approachable colours.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility complaints:</strong> If users report they cannot read your text or distinguish your buttons, the palette is failing its primary function.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Colour Psychology by Industry</h2>

  <p>While colour associations are culturally influenced, certain patterns hold strongly within industries:</p>

  <h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
  <p>Red stimulates appetite and creates urgency — which is why Coca-Cola, McDonald's, and KFC all use it prominently. Yellow evokes happiness and energy, pairing naturally with red (think McDonald's golden arches). Green signals freshness and health, dominating organic and plant-based brands.</p>

  <h3>Finance and Technology</h3>
  <p>Blue dominates finance because it communicates trust, stability, and competence. Barclays, PayPal, and Facebook all use blue as a primary colour. In technology, blue pairs with white and grey to create clean, modern interfaces. The exceptions — like Stripe's purple gradient or Monzo's hot coral — succeed precisely because they break category conventions.</p>

  <h3>Luxury and Fashion</h3>
  <p>Black, gold, and deep burgundy dominate luxury. Chanel, Gucci, and Prada use black as a primary brand colour because it signals exclusivity and timelessness. Tiffany & Co. famously trademarked its robin's-egg blue (Pantone 1837), demonstrating how a single colour can become inseparable from a brand identity.</p>

  <h2>Competitive Analysis Framework</h2>

  <p>Before finalising your palette, audit your competitors systematically:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Collect:</strong> Screenshot the homepages of your top 10 competitors.</li>
    <li><strong>Extract:</strong> Use a colour picker tool to identify each competitor's dominant, secondary, and accent colours.</li>
    <li><strong>Map:</strong> Plot the dominant colours on a colour wheel. Are they clustered in one area?</li>
    <li><strong>Identify gaps:</strong> Where on the wheel is unoccupied? That is your opportunity for differentiation.</li>
    <li><strong>Test contrast:</strong> Ensure your chosen colours are distinct enough from competitors to avoid confusion.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>This exercise takes 30 minutes and often reveals surprising patterns. You may discover that every competitor in your space uses variants of the same blue, leaving orange or teal wide open.</p>

  <h2>Accessibility-First Palette Design</h2>

  <p>An estimated 1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women have colour vision deficiency. Designing palettes without accessibility excludes a significant portion of your audience:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimum contrast:</strong> WCAG 2.1 Level AA requires 4.5:1 contrast for normal text and 3:1 for large text (18pt+ or 14pt+ bold).</li>
    <li><strong>Never rely on colour alone:</strong> Use icons, labels, or patterns alongside colour to convey meaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Simulate colour blindness:</strong> Test your palette with protanopia, deuteranopia, and tritanopia filters. Tools like Stark and Sim Daltonism make this easy.</li>
    <li><strong>Check text on coloured backgrounds:</strong> White text on light yellow fails for everyone, not just colour-blind users.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Real Brand Palette Case Studies</h2>

  <h3>Spotify: From Green to Multi-Colour</h3>
  <p>Spotify launched with a distinctive lime green (#1DB954) that stood out in a music industry dominated by red and black. As the brand matured, Spotify introduced duotone imagery — overlays in varying colours on top of artist photos. This created a flexible system where the core green remained constant but the visual expression adapted to content. The result: instant recognition across contexts without visual monotony.</p>

  <h3>Airbnb: The Belonging Gradient</h3>
  <p>Airbnb's 2014 rebrand replaced the blue wordmark with a warm coral-red "Belo" symbol. The shift from corporate blue to hospitality red reflected a strategic pivot from "find accommodation" to "belong anywhere." The rebrand cost an estimated $100,000 in design fees but contributed to a 30% increase in bookings over the following year — demonstrating that palette changes can drive measurable business outcomes when they align with strategic positioning.</p>

  <h2>Evolving Your Palette Over Time</h2>

  <p>Brands rarely keep the same palette forever. The key is evolution, not revolution:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sub-brand palettes:</strong> Google uses different accent colours for each product (Gmail red, Drive green, Calendar blue) while maintaining the primary brand colours. This creates coherence at the corporate level and differentiation at the product level.</li>
    <li><strong>Seasonal campaigns:</strong> Limited-time colour shifts for holidays or product launches can refresh perception without diluting the core identity. Starbucks' red holiday cups are a classic example — instantly recognisable as temporary.</li>
    <li><strong>Gradual modernisation:</strong> Rather than abrupt rebrands, many companies shift saturation and brightness over years. Compare Apple's logo colours from 1976 (rainbow) to 1998 (monochrome) to 2024 (subtle gradients). The evolution took decades.</li>
  </ul>

