  <h2>Common Palette Mistakes to Avoid</h2>

  <p>Even experienced designers fall into predictable traps when selecting brand colours:</p>

  <h3>Following Trends Blindly</h3>
  <p>The 2010s saw every startup adopt a flat, pastel palette. The 2020s brought neo-brutalism with high-contrast black and neon. Trends expire; your brand should not. If your palette looks dated within three years, you chose fashion over strategy. A timeless palette — think IBM blue, Coca-Cola red, Tiffany blue — outlasts decades of design trends.</p>

  <h3>Ignoring Accessibility from Day One</h3>
  <p>Retrofitting accessibility onto an existing palette is expensive and compromises the design. Start with WCAG AA contrast ratios as a constraint, not an afterthought. The most beautiful inaccessible palette is still a failure.</p>

  <h3>Too Many Colours</h3>
  <p>Every additional colour dilutes brand recognition and increases decision fatigue. Apple's entire product and marketing palette effectively uses three colours: white, black, and occasional accent tints. Restraint is not boring — it is disciplined.</p>

  <h3>Forgetting Print Compatibility</h3>
  <p>A neon green that pops on a Retina screen becomes muddy in CMYK. A vibrant orange on LCD turns brown on newsprint. Always test your final palette in both RGB and CMYK before committing. The cost of reprinting stationery because your colours shifted is far higher than the cost of a proper Pantone match at the outset.</p>

  <h2>Conclusion</h2>

  <p>Choosing a brand palette is not art for art's sake — it is strategic communication. A systematic approach grounded in brand personality, audience understanding, competitive differentiation, and accessibility compliance will serve you far better than chasing trends. Document your choices, test thoroughly across media, and let your colours do some of the talking for you.</p>

